---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import SemesterSpine from "../components/SemesterSpine.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A readback loop drawn as a diagram: two stations, a carrier arcing out from one and back from the other, over a band of clipped waveform, in flat gold and black"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    Aviation radio English is a language built on purpose, by identifiable
    people, to make one kind of mistake impossible. It is worse than ordinary
    English at nearly everything, and that is the trade.
  </p>

  <p>
    This course reads it as a designed artefact. For every rule: what problem
    was it for, what was the alternative, what evidence decided it — and does
    the evidence still hold. The accident record is the other half, and it is
    not here for drama. It is the only evidence available about where a
    language engineered against misunderstanding still lets misunderstanding
    through.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>

  <p>
    You will be on a simulated frequency in week 1 and every week after it. You
    will work the controller position with errors planted in the readbacks and
    find out, as a number, how many you confirm as correct. You will read
    primary accident reports rather than summaries of them, and map their
    findings onto the phrases that now exist — the interesting rows are the ones
    that map onto nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then you will take the reasoning somewhere it has not been done for you. The
    largest piece of work in the course is an
    <a href={withBase("/assessments/phraseology-audit/")}>audit of a spoken
      protocol outside aviation</a> — a clinical handover, a signaller's
    exchange, the
    conference bridge on a major outage — and a phraseology designed for it,
    with an honest account of how your own proposal could make things worse.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <p>
    First-year students, no prerequisites, no aviation background assumed and
    none rewarded. If you can hear the difference between two similar words you
    have everything you need on day one.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is a level 1 course deliberately. Close-reading a transmission, and
    noticing when a protocol has quietly stopped being followed, is foundational
    literacy for anyone who will work on a safety-critical channel — which by
    graduation is most people. Filing it higher would concede it is a curiosity.
  </p>

  <p>
    You should probably not take it if you want to learn to fly: this is a
    course about language, and it will not help you pass a flight test. You
    might want it if you build systems where people talk to each other under
    pressure, or if you have ever watched a handover go wrong and been unable to
    say exactly what went wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>The twelve weeks</h2>

  <p>
    The lecture argues, the sim session drills, and four times a semester
    something is due.
  </p>

  <SemesterSpine />

  <h2>How it is marked</h2>

  <p>
    Four components. A small early drill to establish you can operate the thing
    being argued about, a transcript analysis, the audit, and ten minutes on a
    live frequency in week 12. Accent, fluency and speed are not criteria in any
    of them, and asking for a repeat counts in your favour — the
    <a href={withBase("/assessments/frequency-check/")}>frequency check page</a>
    says why at length.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>

  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>
        The twelve weeks, from what the simplex channel takes away to what a
        designed language cannot fix.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        What actually happens in the hour on frequency, week by week, and what
        you are expected to walk out with.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four components, what each is worth, and what none of them mark.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Glossary" href="/glossary/">
      <p>
        The phrases the course holds you to, and the forms it teaches you to
        hear as wrong.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
